# a11y capture: yt-list-scroll-3-gestures
# device: Pixel 3 (192.168.1.68:5555), Android 12 / API 31. Captured 2026-09-11.
# HOW: YouTube force-stopped, relaunched, its 3-second DELAY overlay left to auto-complete
#      untouched, the comments of a feed item opened by hand, then EXACTLY THREE slow scroll
#      gestures inside that list, 1.5s apart. Screen content screenshot-verified before capture --
#      checking mCurrentFocus alone is NOT enough inside YouTube's single-activity shell, which is
#      how three earlier attempts silently captured a video player instead. Driven by the
#      device-tester agent; YouTube rather than Instagram because Instagram and TikTok are never
#      driven over adb (tools/qa/ig-walkthrough-capture.sh).
# EXPECTED (test oracle): 8. The user moved past eight items in three gestures. NOT 21, which is
#      how many events those three gestures emitted, and which is what the old rule counted.
# WHAT IT PROVES: issue #28's "scrolling the comments counts ~10 taps continuously", measured.
#      Three human gestures produced TWENTY-ONE TYPE_VIEW_SCROLLED events in 4.6 seconds. fromIndex
#      across the stream reads 2,3,4,4,4,4,4,5,6,7,7,7,7,7,8,9,10,10,10,10,10 -- eight forward
#      transitions. Counting transitions scores the items; counting events scores the gesture's
#      duration.
#
# IMPORTANT, and the reason this file is NOT named "comments-sheet": on YouTube the comments list
# and the home feed carry the SAME viewIdResourceName (com.google.android.youtube:id/results) and
# the same class (android.support.v7.widget.RecyclerView) -- device-verified against the feed
# captures from the same session. They appear to be one reused list surface rather than a sheet
# over a feed, so on YouTube the counter cannot and should not try to tell them apart; it counts
# whichever list the user is scrolling, which is the honest answer. The feed-versus-sheet
# separation InteractionCounter implements is for screens that really do have two sources, which
# instagram-idle-no-input.jsonl shows Instagram does (android:id/list alongside
# com.instagram.android:id/swipeable_tab_view_pager in one window).
#
# NOTE: no counter RULE was active on this surface (the whole-app DELAY rule stops applying once
#      feature=SHORTS is detected), so this capture cannot show what Nudge's counter DID -- only
#      what the event stream was. The counting is asserted by replay instead.
